  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef's bakery sells NNN types of cakes, numbered from 111 to NNN.\n\nThe iii-th type of cake has a weight of iii units.\n\nThe bakery just received an order: a customer would like to buy **exactly** KKK cakes with a **total weight** that equals SSS units.\n\nThere are no other restrictions - that is, the order can include any number of cakes of each type, as long as there are exactly KKK cakes with their total weight being SSS.\n\nIt is guaranteed that K≤S≤K⋅NK \\le S \\le K\\cdot NK≤S≤K⋅N, so that there always exists a way to fulfill the order.\n\nUnfortunately, the bakery is running low on ingredients for only the cake of type MMM.\n\nSo, Chef would like to minimize the number of type-MMM cakes included in the order.\n\nYour task is thus to tell Chef the **minimum** number of type-MMM cakes that must be included in the order.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single lines of input, containing four space-separated integers N,K,S,N, K, S,N,K,S, and MMM.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum number of type MMM cakes needed to fulfill the order.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N,K≤2⋅1051 \\leq N, K \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N,K≤2⋅105",
      "K≤S≤K⋅NK \\le S \\le K\\cdot NK≤S≤K⋅N",
      "1≤M≤N1 \\le M \\le N1≤M≤N",
      "The sum of NNN and the sum of KKK over all test cases each won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n4 2 5 3\n3 3 6 2\n100000 100000 10000000000 100000",
        "output": "0\n1\n100000",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We have N=4,K=2,S=5,M=3N = 4, K = 2, S = 5, M = 3N=4,K=2,S=5,M=3.\nOne option for the order is to include one cake with weight 111 and one cake with weight 444.\nThis makes two cakes with a total weight of 1+4=5=S1+4=5=S1+4=5=S.\nNo cakes of weight 333 are used, so the minimum number of uses is 000 which is optimal.\nTest case 222: We have N=3,K=3,S=6,M=2N = 3, K = 3, S = 6, M = 2N=3,K=3,S=6,M=2.\nThe only possible option is to include one cake of weights 1,2,31, 2, 31,2,3, for a total of 666.\nIt can be verified that no other combination of three weights in [1,3][1, 3][1,3] can give a total weight of 666.\nSo, we must always use 111 cake with weight 222, and the answer is 111.\nTest case 333: We have N=K=M=105N = K = M = 10^5N=K=M=105 and S=1010S = 10^{10}S=1010.\nThe only possible option is to have 10510^5105 cakes of weight 10510^5105 each, so the answer is 10510^5105."
      }
    ]
  },
